# Critical architectural principle

The core trading systems should not know whether they are operating in:

```text
Simulator
Historical replay
Fake broker test
Broker Practice/Demo
Future broker Live
```

The following systems should consume normalized data, immutable configuration and broker-neutral contracts:

```text
Agent
ChartAnnotator
RiskManager
PortfolioManager
ExecutionManager
TradeManager
Calibration/meta-label pipeline
Trading-condition filter
Account-safety logic
```

Environment-specific knowledge should remain at adapter and orchestration boundaries such as:

```text
Simulator
LiveTradingHost
broker-specific live adapter
broker-specific networking project
historical data adapters
broker stream adapters
simulation execution/fill adapters
```

Verify this principle from the actual project references, namespaces, models and call paths.

Look for indirect leakage as well as obvious references.

Examples of indirect environment leakage include:

- `IsLive`, `IsDemo`, `IsSimulation` flags inside domain models;
- live-only modes attached to neutral trade candidates;
- broker identifiers embedded in Agent decisions;
- simulator fill assumptions inside risk or trade management;
- separate live and simulator Agent configurations;
- different construction paths producing subtly different strategies;
- live-specific clock logic inside the Agent;
- strategy behaviour changing because of host mode rather than policy;
- direct broker calls from Agents or TradeManager;
- domain services reading environment variables;
- Dashboard input silently overriding promoted research policy.

